==============================================================================
THE URAM ESCAPE — spilling the DeltaNet state to DDR, costed
==============================================================================
    The DN state is touched ONCE per token per layer, so streaming it
    from DDR costs 2x its size in traffic per token (read + write).
    model   state MiB  traffic MiB/tok  weights MiB (W8)  as % of weights
    2B            9.0             18.0            1847.2             1.0 %   (W4: 1.9 %)
    4B           24.0             48.0            4104.2             1.2 %   (W4: 2.3 %)
    9B           24.0             48.0            7686.2             0.6 %   (W4: 1.2 %)
    -> the BANDWIDTH cost of spilling is small; the cost is a new DMA
       path in dn_step/layer_chan and a state-coherency protocol, which
       is a large RTL project, not a parameter change.

==============================================================================
THE OTHER URAM LEVER — narrowing the DN state element from int16
==============================================================================
    The DN state row is 2048 b = 128 columns x 16 b (rtl/layer_chan.sv:424, rtl/dn_step.sv:12).
    The KV cache next to it is ALREADY 8 b/element (2048 b / 256 head_dim).
    At int8 two state rows pack into one 2048-bit URAM row, halving
    the bank count.  This is a FIDELITY change with no measurement
    anywhere in this repo — it is named as a lever, not proposed.
    4B   DN state int16 (as built) DN  696 + KV 232 =  928 URAM   96.7% of 960  3 SLRs
    4B   DN state int8             DN  348 + KV 232 =  580 URAM   60.4% of 960  2 SLRs
    9B   DN state int16 (as built) DN  696 + KV 232 =  928 URAM   96.7% of 960  3 SLRs
    9B   DN state int8             DN  348 + KV 232 =  580 URAM   60.4% of 960  2 SLRs

